When the HTML parser needs a byte that a late encoding declaration
would change, Ladybird first looks ahead in the bytes received for one,
as far as the end of the head. Blink and WebKit look ahead the same
way, holding back decoding meanwhile: Blink’s HTMLMetaCharsetParser::
CheckForMetaCharset() tokenizes ahead until a tag that can’t be in the
head, once at least 1024 bytes are in. WebKit’s TextResourceDecoder::
checkForHeadCharset() does the same. Gecko’s nsHtml5StreamParser keeps
looking for a meta charset until the head ends past the first kilobyte
(TemplatePushedOrHeadPopped()), and decodes the buffered bytes again
with the declared encoding. Ladybird stops at the end of the head by the
tree builder’s rules, without also scanning on to the 1024th byte, since
the prescan has covered those bytes already.
